  "essay": "In 1959, a 20-year-old University of Maryland student named John Fahey pressed 100 copies of a solo steel-string guitar record and drove around Washington, D.C. slipping them onto the shelves of used record stores himself, hoping somebody would mistake them for a genuine rediscovered blues 78. One side was credited to Fahey; the other to a fictional bluesman he'd invented called 'Blind Joe Death.' He called his self-run label Takoma Records, after his hometown of Takoma Park, Maryland.\n\nThat one weird, obsessive gesture created an entire lineage — 'American Primitive': solo acoustic guitar as a vehicle for something between blues, Americana, Indian raga, and 20th-century classical composition. It went underground for decades and kept resurfacing in exactly the corners of music Buster already loves: Drag City's roster, Chicago post-rock, and the current generation of Appalachian/Virginia guitarists.\n\nThis playlist traces that bloodline from the source (Fahey, Robbie Basho, Sandy Bull) through the 1990s Chicago revival that plugged Fahey back into the Drag City ecosystem (Gastr del Sol, Cul de Sac's actual collaboration with Fahey), into the Louisville/Drag City guitar tradition Buster already knows from Papa M, and finally out to the 2000s-2020s revivalists — Jack Rose, Six Organs' Ben Chasny, Glenn Jones (who played with Fahey directly), Nashville's William Tyler (who also played in Silver Jews), Nathan Salsburg, Marisa Anderson, Steve Gunn, and Daniel Bachman.\n\nDeliberately vocal-free — every track is instrumental guitar, unusual for these playlists but true to the genre."
